\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Deterministic matching can fail even when two datasets contain the same individuals because it requires exact agreement on every field. Any variation, a typo in a name, a transposed digit in a birth year, or a ZIP code change from moving \'97 causes a true match to be missed. This creates a fundamental trade-off between two types of error. False matches (false positives) occur when records belonging to different individuals are incorrectly linked, inflating the matched set with wrong pairs. Missed matches (false negatives) occur when records belonging to the same individual fail to link, shrinking the matched set and losing valid pairs. Deterministic matching effectively sets an extreme threshold: it accepts zero tolerance for field-level disagreement, which minimizes false matches but maximizes missed matches whenever the data contain any noise.\
Probabilistic matching manages this trade-off by computing a similarity score for each candidate pair rather than requiring exact agreement. Fields are compared using measures like Jaro-Winkler string similarity, and the model estimates a posterior probability that each pair is a true match. The researcher then chooses a threshold: a high threshold reduces false matches but increases missed matches, while a low threshold captures more true matches at the cost of admitting more false ones. This lets the researcher explicitly navigate the trade-off rather than being locked into the all-or-nothing logic of exact matching.\
